<Window x:Class="COT_VCF.Presentation.HelpWindow" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation" xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="COT VCF Parametric Automation — Help" Icon="{StaticResource AppLogo}" Width="860" Height="760" MinWidth="560" MinHeight="420"
        Background="{StaticResource Background}" WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner" ShowInTaskbar="False">
 <Window.Resources>
  <Style TargetType="Paragraph"><Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,8"/><Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="19"/></Style>
  <Style x:Key="H1" TargetType="Paragraph"><Setter Property="FontSize" Value="18"/><Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/><Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,10"/></Style>
  <Style x:Key="H2" TargetType="Paragraph"><Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14"/><Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/><Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,14,0,6"/><Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource Accent}"/></Style>
  <Style x:Key="Formula" TargetType="Paragraph"><Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource MonoFont}"/><Setter Property="Margin" Value="18,2,0,8"/><Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource Surface}"/><Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,4"/></Style>
  <Style TargetType="List"><Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,8"/><Setter Property="Padding" Value="20,0,0,0"/></Style>
  <Style TargetType="FlowDocument"><Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="Segoe UI"/><Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12.5"/><Setter Property="PagePadding" Value="22,16"/><Setter Property="Background" Value="White"/></Style>
 </Window.Resources>
 <TabControl x:Name="Topics" Margin="8" TabStripPlacement="Top">
  <TabControl.Resources>
   <Style TargetType="TabItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type TabItem}}">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="28"/>
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="130"/>
   </Style>
  </TabControl.Resources>

  <!-- ===================== WORKFLOW ===================== -->
  <TabItem Header="Workflow">
   <FlowDocumentScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
    <FlowDocument>
     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H1}">Workflow</Paragraph>
     <List MarkerStyle="Decimal">
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Connect to a running SAP2000 model from the top bar and load geometry.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Tab 01: select Point Objects in SAP2000, add them as applied points, and define loading criteria.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Tab 02: select level chord groups, capture a baseline and preview height changes.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Tab 03: select SAP cable objects and define PRETENSION target-force candidates.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Tab 04: define deflection points, member-force groups, foundation reactions, and monitored cables/result case. No SAP group write occurs while editing.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Tab 05: select study rows and 1-3 total SAP2000 instances. Run first prepares [O]- groups on the parent baseline and saves it, then workers create children from that prepared parent.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Each worker is sequential; workers run in parallel and reopen the immutable baseline before every candidate.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Tab 07 FEM: an internal 2D analysis of the full Tab 01-03 candidate space without running SAP2000 — see the FEM procedure topic.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>
     <Paragraph>Viewer: mouse wheel zooms, drag pans, double-click or Home fits. With FEM results shown, hover over a member to read the section result.</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>Each child runs the load cases enabled in SAP2000, is verified, and its monitored-point deflection is extracted for Tab 06.</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>A candidate whose child model already exists (e.g. from an overlapping study mode) is reused — opened, verified and re-extracted — never rebuilt or overwritten. Delete its iteration folder to force a rebuild.</Paragraph>
    </FlowDocument>
   </FlowDocumentScrollViewer>
  </TabItem>

  <!-- ===================== FEM PROCEDURE ===================== -->
  <TabItem Header="FEM procedure">
   <FlowDocumentScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
    <FlowDocument>
     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H1}">Tab 07 FEM — analysis procedure</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>The FEM tab analyses every Tab 01 loading × Tab 02 height × Tab 03 pretension combination with an internal two-dimensional
      frame solver. It reads section and material data from the connected SAP2000 model but never writes to it, and it does not run SAP2000.
      Units are kN and m throughout.</Paragraph>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">1. Structural model</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Plane:</Bold> all analysed joints must lie in one XZ plane (Y within 0.1 µm). Three degrees of freedom per joint: UX, UZ and rotation RY.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Frames:</Bold> linear-elastic Euler–Bernoulli beam-columns. A, E and the in-plane I come from the assigned SAP section and material,
       including SAP property modifiers (A × m1, I22 × m5, I33 × m6) and the frame's local-axis orientation. SAP end-moment releases (M3 at I and/or J) are honoured.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Cables:</Bold> tension-only axial members, stiffness EA/L. A cable never carries compression: when its force would become negative it goes slack and is removed from the stiffness.
       With <Italic>Cable sag</Italic> on (Analysis, default), a cable that a load case loads along its length hangs in a shallow parabola instead — see section 5a.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Supports:</Bold> pins detected automatically at the lowest joints of near-vertical frame members; UX and UZ fixed, rotation free.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Mass and self-weight:</Bold> unit weight γ and mass ρ come from the SAP material; self-weight per length = γ × gross A × weight modifier
       (mass = ρ × A × mass modifier, shown on Inputs → Sectional properties). Frame stiffness uses A × m1 and I × m5/m6; cable stiffness uses A × the cable area modifier.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Loads:</Bold> see section 3a — SAP load patterns, cases and combinations, plus the Tab 01 study loads.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Theory:</Bold> small displacements — no P-Δ and no large-displacement geometry update. The one geometric nonlinearity
       included is the sag of the cables (section 5a).</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">2. Frame splitting and meshing</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>As in SAP2000, a frame is first split at every member-end joint that lies on it (within 1 mm), so verticals and cable anchors along a
      full-length chord connect to it. Each piece is then divided into N equal sub-elements (Analysis → <Italic>Frame mesh</Italic>, default 10).
      Cables are never split or meshed.</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>The interior mesh nodes carry no load and connect only to their frame, so they are statically condensed onto the frame's end joints:</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource Formula}">K_condensed = K_ee − K_ei · K_ii⁻¹ · K_ie</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>This is exact and keeps the global system the size of the original model. After the solve, the mesh-node displacements are recovered
      (u_i = −K_ii⁻¹ K_ie u_e) and give the axial force, shear, moment and displacement at every station along the frame — used by the diagrams,
      the curved deflected shape and the hover read-out. For joint loads only, meshing does not change joint displacements or member-end forces.</Paragraph>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">3. Candidate cases</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>Case IDs combine the three axes, e.g. <Run FontFamily="{StaticResource MonoFont}">L02_H03_P05</Run> = loading row 2, height 3, pretension combination 5.
      For each case the Tab 02 chord offsets are applied to the baseline geometry, then the two stages below are solved.</Paragraph>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">3a. Load patterns, load cases and combinations (Inputs → Loads)</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph><Italic>Read from SAP</Italic> imports, read-only, the model's load patterns (type and self-weight multiplier), every load assigned to the analysed
      joints, frames and cables, its static load cases and its response combinations. They can then be edited here — self-weight multiplier, case
      definition (e.g. <Run FontFamily="{StaticResource MonoFont}">1 DEAD + 1 PT</Run>), combination factors and which to run — without changing SAP.</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Pattern loads:</Bold> joint F1, F3 and M2 (global); frame distributed and point loads in local 1/2, global X/Z, projected X/Z and
       gravity directions; SAP gravity-load assignments (multiples of self-weight); cable distributed and gravity loads (lumped to the cable ends).
       Out-of-plane components, moment loads on members and non-global coordinate systems are ignored and listed as warnings.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Member loads on the mesh:</Bold> converted to consistent nodal loads on every mesh node (Hermite shape functions, integrated exactly),
       condensed with the stiffness, and subtracted as fixed-end forces when the station forces are recovered — so diagrams show the true load effect along the member.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Tab 01 study loads:</Bold> in a Tab 01 load pattern, SAP joint forces at the applied points are replaced by the candidate's loads, as the SAP study writes them.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Load cases:</Bold> each linear or nonlinear static case is solved as its own tension-only equilibrium. A nonlinear case that
       continues from another includes that case's loads (staged). A case that includes a Tab 03 target-force pattern carries the fitted pretension × its factor;
       a case without it has no cable pretension. (SAP treats cables in a linear case as linear; here cables are always tension-only.)</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Combinations:</Bold> linear additive combinations are evaluated as the factored sum of case (or nested combination) results, as SAP does.
       Envelope, absolute, SRSS and range combinations are listed but not evaluated.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>If no SAP case uses the Tab 03 or Tab 01 patterns, the app adds <Run FontFamily="{StaticResource MonoFont}">FEM_PT</Run> (target-force pattern alone)
       and <Run FontFamily="{StaticResource MonoFont}">FEM_TAB01</Run> (Tab 01 loads, continuing from the pretension case) so the study loads always act on the prestressed structure.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>On Results, <Italic>Load case / combo</Italic> chooses which result the table, viewer, hover read-out and the Cable target fit Result column show.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">4. Stage 1 — cable pretension fitting (SAP "Target Force")</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>Each Tab 03 value is a <Italic>target</Italic> tension P_preset, not a load. SAP2000 meets a Target Force at the end of the load case in which
      the target-force pattern is applied, so the solver finds the applied pretensions P_applied that produce the targets under <Bold>the other loads of that case</Bold>
      (none, for a pretension-only case such as FEM_PT; e.g. self-weight if the case also holds DEAD):</Paragraph>
     <List MarkerStyle="Decimal">
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Run 1: apply P_applied = P_preset to every cable and solve. Because the frame shortens under the pretension, P_result is usually below the target.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Build the response matrix J (how each cable's tension changes per kN of pretension in every cable) by finite differences — this captures the interaction between cables.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Correct all pretensions together: ΔP = J⁺ · (P_preset − P_result) (minimum-norm, so cable segments in series share the correction evenly); pretension is kept ≥ 0.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Repeat until every cable satisfies the tolerance, or until <Italic>Max iterations</Italic> runs (default 100).</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>
     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource Formula}">| P_result / P_preset − 1 | ≤ tolerance (Analysis → Target-force fitting ±, default 10 %)</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>The pretension-only response is nearly linear, so this Newton procedure normally converges in 2–4 runs. If the targets cannot all be
      met — for example opposing cables whose targets would need a negative (compressive) pretension in one of them — the closest result is kept, the case is
      marked <Italic>Not fitted</Italic> with the reason, and the run continues.</Paragraph>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">5. Stage 2 — loaded equilibrium</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>Every load case is then solved with its loads and its share of the fitted pretension. The tension-only problem is solved by a damped
      Newton method minimising total potential energy:</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource Formula}">Π(u) = ½ uᵀK_frames u + Σ_cables T(u)² / (2·EA/L) − fᵀu,   T(u) = max(0, P_applied + EA/L · Δℓ(u))</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Each iteration re-checks which cables are taut, solves for a step, then halves it (line search) until the energy decreases.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Slack cables keep a tiny stiffness (10⁻⁶ EA/L) in the step matrix only, and a growing diagonal (Levenberg) shift is tried if a step fails —
       this keeps joints held only by slack or collinear cables solvable. Residual and energy use the true stiffness, so the converged answer is unchanged.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Converged when the largest free-DOF residual force ≤ 10⁻⁷ × the largest load or pretension (max 100 iterations). Because the energy is convex,
       the result does not depend on load path, so "pretension then load" is solved in one step.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>
     <Paragraph>A case that does not reach equilibrium is listed as <Italic>Failed</Italic> with its reason; the remaining cases still run.</Paragraph>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">5a. Cable sag (Analysis → Cable sag, on by default)</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>A cable that a load case loads along its length — its self-weight, when the case includes a pattern with a self-weight multiplier, or a
      distributed load assigned to it — does not stay straight. It hangs in a shallow parabola, so its curve is longer than its chord and part of any
      joint movement goes into changing the sag instead of stretching the cable. With w_n the load normal to the chord (self-weight w gives w·cos θ),
      L the chord length and k = EA/L, the tension T is the positive root of</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource Formula}">T / k − a / T² = Δℓ(u) + P_applied / k,   a = w_n²·L³ / 24</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>and the cable's energy in Π(u) becomes T²/2k − 2a/T. From this follow:</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Stiffness:</Bold> dT/dΔℓ = k / (1 + w_n²·L²·EA / 12T³) — Ernst's equivalent modulus. Near 100 % of EA/L for a highly tensioned cable,
       a small fraction for a long or lightly tensioned one. The cable table shows it as <Italic>Stiff. %</Italic>.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Sag:</Bold> f = w_n·L² / 8T at mid-span, shown as <Italic>Sag mm</Italic> and in the hover read-out.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>No slack switch:</Bold> the root is always positive. Where a straight cable would go slack, the sagging cable keeps the tension its
       weight needs to hang across the shortened chord.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Pretension:</Bold> P_applied keeps its meaning — the lack of fit of the cable × EA/L. Target-force fitting adjusts it until the
       sagging cable's tension meets the target under the loads of the pretension stage, so the fitted P_applied is a little lower than for a straight cable.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Unchanged:</Bold> a cable with no load along it in that case, or a vertical cable under gravity (w_n = 0), is the straight
       tension-only cable. The cable's load still reaches its end joints exactly as before; sag only changes the cable's own force law. Joint displacements
       remain small-displacement and frames remain linear. The energy stays convex, so the solution is still unique and independent of load path.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>
     <Paragraph>Switch <Italic>Cable sag</Italic> off to compare with straight tension-only cables.</Paragraph>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">6. Run monitor and convergence chart</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>Run Analysis opens the run monitor (reopen it with <Italic>Monitor</Italic>). It shows progress, a live log of each step and case, and the
      convergence chart for the cable chosen under <Italic>Cable</Italic> (default Cable1a-2):</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>x-axis — number of run (pretension-fitting iteration); run 0 is the unstressed state;</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>y-axis — P_result / P_preset; the line at 1 is the target and the shaded band is the ± tolerance.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>
     <Paragraph>The chart follows each case as it completes; choose a <Italic>Case</Italic> to inspect a particular one.</Paragraph>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">7. Results and sign conventions</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Cable target fit</Bold> (Analysis): Target, Pretension (stage-1 tension), Dev. %, Status, Result (final tension under load), Applied P and fitting runs.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Axial force</Bold>: + tension, − compression. Cable tensions are labelled "T … kN".</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Bending moment and shear</Bold>: one convention along each frame from I to J, continuous across joints.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Reactions</Bold>: Rx (+ global X) and Rz (+ up) shown separately.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Displacements</Bold>: ux, uz in the global axes; the deflected shape is magnified for display only.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">8. Limitations — check critical results in SAP2000</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Planar, linear-elastic, small-displacement analysis; no P-Δ or buckling. Cable sag is the shallow-parabola (Ernst) model of
       section 5a, not a full catenary with large displacements: it is accurate while the sag stays below about one eighth of the span and the joints
       move little compared with the cable length.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Only static load cases and linear additive combinations; no temperature, settlement, moving, modal, response-spectrum or time-history analysis,
       and no moment loads on members. Superposing tension-only cases in a combination is the same approximation SAP makes for combinations of nonlinear cases.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>Tendon objects, partial-fixity releases and non-pinned supports are not modelled.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph>SAP2000 meets Target Force with its own iterative scheme; results should agree in the linear range but are not guaranteed identical.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>

     <Paragraph Style="{StaticResource H2}">9. Basis of the solver — sources and verification</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph>The solver was written for this application. It contains no code from OpenSees, SAP2000 or any other analysis package; the only
      library it uses is MathNet.Numerics for linear algebra. Each part implements a published, standard formulation:</Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Frames</Bold> — direct stiffness method, Euler–Bernoulli beam-column element, end releases by condensation, consistent nodal loads:
       McGuire, Gallagher &amp; Ziemian, <Italic>Matrix Structural Analysis</Italic>, 2nd ed., Wiley, 2000; Cook, Malkus, Plesha &amp; Witt,
       <Italic>Concepts and Applications of Finite Element Analysis</Italic>, 4th ed., Wiley, 2002.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Frame meshing</Bold> — static condensation of the interior nodes: Guyan, "Reduction of stiffness and mass matrices",
       AIAA Journal 3(2), 1965, p. 380.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Cable sag</Bold> — the elastic cable in a shallow parabola: Irvine, <Italic>Cable Structures</Italic>, MIT Press, 1981. Its stiffness is the
       equivalent modulus of Ernst, "Der E-Modul von Seilen unter Berücksichtigung des Durchhanges", Der Bauingenieur 40(2), 1965, pp. 52–55; see also
       Gimsing &amp; Georgakis, <Italic>Cable Supported Bridges</Italic>, 3rd ed., Wiley, 2012.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Tension-only equilibrium</Bold> — minimum of the total potential energy, convex for cables without compression (Kanno,
       <Italic>Nonsmooth Mechanics and Convex Optimization</Italic>, CRC Press, 2011), by Newton's method with a backtracking line search and a Levenberg shift:
       Nocedal &amp; Wright, <Italic>Numerical Optimization</Italic>, 2nd ed., Springer, 2006; Levenberg, Quarterly of Applied Mathematics 2(2), 1944, pp. 164–168.</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Pretension fitting</Bold> — follows the intent of SAP2000's Target-Force load (CSI Analysis Reference Manual) with a Newton iteration
       of its own on the cable pretensions; it is not CSI's algorithm.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>
     <Paragraph><Bold>Verified</Bold> by the application's own checks against closed-form results: mid-span deflection PL³/48EI and 5wL⁴/384EI, moments wL²/8 and
      P·a·b/L, reactions and overall equilibrium, meshed against unmeshed frames, superposition of load cases, and for cable sag an independent solution of
      the cable equation and Ernst's formula (agreement to about 10⁻⁹).</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph><Bold>Benchmarked against SAP2000</Bold> on a completed study (COT Framing_V1.2, 135 candidates), like for like: the FEM is run on SAP2000's own
      text copy of the model, on the same candidates, with the cable target forces met in the same case (<Italic>Pretension</Italic>, which includes
      self-weight) and compared in the same staged load cases. The total vertical reaction agrees to 0.002 %; cable forces to within 3.8 % and
      deflections to within 3.2 % of the largest SAP2000 value (mean differences about 600 kN and 2 mm); support reactions to within 1.2 %. What
      remains is the difference in theory: SAP2000 analysed those cases with P-Delta / large displacements, catenary cables and shear-deformable
      frames. The comparison is a check of the application (<Italic>--fem-sap</Italic>) and can be re-run on another study. Not compared with OpenSees.</Paragraph>
     <Paragraph><Bold>Two things that benchmark found — read them before comparing the FEM tab with a SAP2000 study:</Bold></Paragraph>
     <List>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>Composite Section Designer sections weigh too much in the FEM tab.</Bold> SAP2000 reports the area of such a section transformed to
       its base material, and the FEM's SAP read multiplies that by the base material's unit weight. A concrete-filled steel box of 105 kN/m becomes
       150 kN/m. On the benchmark model the FEM tab carries 4 % more load than SAP2000. (The benchmark itself uses the correct weights.)</Paragraph></ListItem>
      <ListItem><Paragraph><Bold>SAP2000 studies run before this was fixed kept the baseline cable lengths.</Bold> The study changes the truss height by moving
       joints. The ends of a cable move with them, but a SAP2000 cable object defined by its undeformed length keeps that number — SAP2000's text copy
       of each child model shows it. In a taller candidate SAP2000 then analysed cables whose undeformed length was up to 29 % shorter than the chord
       they span — up to 41 % stiffer — and gave smaller deflections than the FEM tab (on the benchmark: equal at the baseline height, about −25 % at
       the tallest). The study now gives every such cable the same length relative to its chord when it moves the joints; on the tallest candidate
       SAP2000 and the FEM tab then agree within 0.2 % on deflection. Candidates above the baseline height from an earlier study have to be run again.</Paragraph></ListItem>
     </List>
    </FlowDocument>
   </FlowDocumentScrollViewer>
  </TabItem>
 </TabControl>
</Window>
